## 1. Introduction

### 1.1 Purpose

This PRD defines **what** the product must do, **for whom**, and **how we will know it is done**. It does not define how the product is built; that belongs to the TRD and the build guides. Every requirement here has an ID, a priority and a testable acceptance criterion, so that all four team members build toward the same target without needing to ask each other.

### 1.2 Product in One Paragraph

The product detects cyber intrusions, reconstructs how they spread through a network, and, through its novel **Precog Engine**, forecasts where the attacker will most likely go next and lets an analyst pre-emptively contain that predicted path before the next step happens. It runs in **Replay Mode**: realistic pre-recorded and scripted attack events are replayed through the same live pipeline that real traffic would use. The analyst sees the result on a 3D console (laptop) and on an installable mobile web app (phone).

### 1.3 Audience

| Reader | What they use this PRD for |
|---|---|
| LEAD-CORE, DETECT, GRAPH, VIZ | The single source of truth for what to build and when a piece is done |
| Guide and evaluators | Understanding the product scope, the evaluation approach and the demo |
| TRD and build-guide authors | The requirement IDs they must trace back to |

### 1.4 Product Name

The project's working title is the full title above. The novel feature is called the **Precog Engine**. No separate marketing name has been given, so none is invented here.

