### 1.6 Glossary

| Term | Meaning |
|---|---|
| **SOC** | Security Operations Center: the team or room that watches for and responds to cyber attacks |
| **Analyst** | The person using the product to watch for and respond to attacks |
| **Node / Host** | One machine in the monitored network, shown as a point on the graph |
| **Edge** | An observed connection between two hosts |
| **Attack graph** | The live picture of which hosts are compromised and how the attack moved between them |
| **Compromised node** | A host the detection engine has confirmed as attacked successfully |
| **Replay Mode** | Running the pipeline from pre-recorded and scripted attack events instead of live attacks |
| **Golden path** | The single rehearsed demo scenario that must work every time |
| **Precog Engine** | The novel feature: predicts the attacker's most likely next targets with confidence scores |
| **Forecast** | The ranked list (Top-3) of likely next targets with confidence percentages |
| **Forecast cone** | The glowing directional shape on the 3D graph pointing from the compromised node toward a predicted target |
| **Containment** | The simulated isolation of a node or segment (a status change plus a logged action) |
| **Re-evaluation** | Feeding the post-containment state back into the pipeline to confirm the attack stopped |
| **Closed loop** | Detect → Forecast → Act → Re-evaluate, repeating |
| **Markov transition model** | A model that estimates, from observed sequences, the probability of moving from one state (node) to the next |
| **precision@k** | The fraction of the top-k forecasts that contain the node actually attacked next |
| **Calibration** | Whether a stated confidence (for example 74%) matches how often that forecast is actually right |
| **False-positive rate** | How often harmless traffic is wrongly flagged as an attack |
| **PWA** | Progressive Web App: a web app that can be installed on a phone's home screen and behaves like an app |
| **MVP** | Minimum Viable Product: the smallest version that still works end to end |
| **MoSCoW** | Priority scale used in this PRD: Must, Should, Could, Won't |
| **Flask** | The Python web framework used by the reused NIDS backend |
| **Polling** | The screen asking the server for updates every second or two, instead of the server pushing them |

