## 2. Product Overview

### 2.1 Problem Statement

Security teams, especially in small organizations without a full SOC, struggle to understand how an intrusion is spreading through their network in real time. By the time the attack path is understood, damage is often already done. This product detects intrusions, reconstructs how they spread, and, via the novel feature, predicts and pre-empts where they are going next.

Three capability gaps make up the problem:

| Gap | Statement |
|---|---|
| **G1** | **Detection alone isn't understanding.** A flood of individual alerts does not show how they relate to one unfolding attack |
| **G2** | **Reconstruction alone is reactive.** An attack-path graph shows only what already happened |
| **G3** | **No accessible tool closes the loop.** Nothing widely available combines detection, path reconstruction, forecasting and one-tap response in a single continuously re-evaluating system that a small team can build and demonstrate |

### 2.2 Existing Systems and the Gap

| Existing system | Category | Limitation |
|---|---|---|
| Bitdefender GravityZone | Endpoint detection and response | Describes what happened; does not forecast what happens next |
| CrowdStrike Falcon | Endpoint detection and response | Strong at detection and response; does not predict the attacker's next move |
| Sekoia.io | Attack-path visualization | Shows the path after compromise is detected, not before |

This product targets the specific gap none of them address: live, node-level, confidence-scored forecasting of the next move, combined with a pre-emptive containment action that feeds back into the same live system.

### 2.3 Product Vision

An analyst opens the console or the phone app and sees an attack unfolding live, sees where it is predicted to go next with a confidence score, taps one button to block that path, and watches the attack stop before it happens, not after.

### 2.4 Core Differentiator: The Closed Loop

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A[Detect] --> B[Forecast]
    B --> C[Act: Pre-emptive Containment]
    C --> D[Re-evaluate]
    D --> A
```

The detection and graph parts are a well-understood category. The novelty and the publishable contribution are the **Precog Engine's forecasting** and the closed loop around it.

### 2.5 Product Context Diagram

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    DS["Replayed attack events<br/>(scripted attack sequences written as packet rows)"] --> P["Pipeline: NIDS database and detector, events, graph, ATT&CK, Precog"]
    P --> API["Backend API (NIDS, extended)"]
