## 10. Evaluation and Metrics Requirements

Security ML work is judged on evaluation rigor, so the evaluation is fixed upfront.

### 10.1 Detection Evaluation (Owner: DETECT)

| ID | Requirement |
|---|---|
| **EV-1** | A proper train and test split, ideally training on CICIDS2017 and testing on a held-out day or attack type, to show generalization |
| **EV-2** | Report precision, recall, F1 and false-positive rate |
| **EV-3** | Handle class imbalance explicitly and state the method |
| **EV-4** | Log every experiment from Week 2 in a simple spreadsheet or tracker |
| **EV-5** | Set numeric thresholds only after the Week 2 baseline exists |

### 10.2 Precog Evaluation (Owner: LEAD-CORE)

| ID | Requirement |
|---|---|
| **EV-6** | Report forecast precision@1 and precision@3 |
| **EV-7** | Report a calibration curve (stated confidence against actual outcome) |
| **EV-8** | Report containment success rate under simulated replay |
| **EV-9** | Show the live session accuracy counter in the product |
| **EV-10** | Compare against the fallback rule-based lookup, where possible, to show what the model adds |

### 10.3 Paper Framing

The paper is scoped around the Precog Engine's forecasting contribution: *"Probabilistic Attack Trajectory Forecasting for Pre-Emptive Network Containment: A Closed-Loop Detect-Forecast-Act Framework."* Before the paper's novelty claim is finalized, a literature search (Google Scholar, IEEE Xplore) shall be run specifically for this mechanism, so the paper can honestly say "to the best of our knowledge".

