| **RK-3** | Scope creep | High / Medium | Scope frozen after Week 3; new ideas written as future work |
| **RK-4** | The live demo fails on the day | Medium / High | Replay Mode removes live-network dependence; localhost demo; recorded backup video |
| **RK-5** | The guide cannot validate technical correctness | Medium / Medium | The team validates correctness through evaluation metrics and threat-model realism |
| **RK-6** | The novel feature turns out to be less novel than assumed | Medium / High | Literature search specific to the Precog mechanism before the paper's claim is finalized |
| **RK-7** | Merge conflicts or a broken `main` | Medium / Medium | Folder per role, branch per person, only LEAD-CORE merges, branch protection |
| **RK-8** | The phone can only install the web app from a hosted HTTPS address, and the backend must be reachable | Medium / Medium | The NIDS Flask app serves the built web app itself, so one free backend host over HTTPS covers both; localhost remains the primary demo |
| **RK-9** | Evaluators expect a native Android app for the Mobile Application Development subject | Medium / Medium | Web first; an APK wrapper and an Android Studio run are available on request |
| **RK-10** | The team has no networking, Linux or cybersecurity background | High / Medium | Replay Mode, reuse of existing repositories, extremely basic build guides |
| **RK-11** | Free tiers or modest laptops are too slow for the 3D console | Medium / Medium | Keep the demo network small; test on the weakest laptop early |
| **RK-12** | Free hosting limits (sleeping services, usage caps) hurt the phone demo | Medium / Medium | Wake and test the hosted services before the demo; fall back to localhost and video |
| **RK-13** | A teammate is blocked waiting for another | Medium / Medium | Contract-first sample data so every role builds alone |
| **RK-14** | NIDS ships with a default admin login, a hardcoded secret key and hardcoded database passwords | High / Medium | Move them to environment variables; never commit real values |
| **RK-15** | NIDS runs its detector in a 30-second batch loop, too slow for live updates | High / Medium | Replay writes events already labelled, or the loop is shortened (FR-DET-7) |
| **RK-16** | NIDS pins older libraries and expects PostgreSQL; the SQLite switch or the pinned libraries may not work | Medium / High | Verify both in Week 1; separate Python environments where versions clash; Docker Compose as the documented fallback |
| **RK-17** | NIDS has no graph, forecast, containment or live push, so add-on code is still needed | High / Medium | Add-ons live in separate files that register routes in the same app; build guides give finished code and prompts |

